  "finalAnswerPreview": "## Decision\\n\\n**The integration should expect Ordarium's `InvalidCursorError` (string code `\\\"INVALID_CURSOR\\\"`) — a fail-closed caller-input failure. It should *not* expect numeric code 27.**\\n\\n## Why\\n\\n**1. The pinned contract defines it.** The workspace depends on `@ordarium/core@1.3.1` (`package.json`). In that artifact:\\n\\n- `node_modules/@ordarium/core/dist/src/errors.d.ts` / `errors.js`: `InvalidCursorError extends OrdariumError` with code `\\\"INVALID_CURSOR\\\"` and message *\\\"The change cursor is not a valid durable position; refusing to guess a resume point.\\\"* Its doc comment is explicit: it is *\\\"Distinct from LEDGER_CORRUPT: this is invalid caller input, not damaged ledger content, and it fails closed instead of silently restarting from the beginning of the feed.\\\"*\\n- Throw sites (both ledger implementations) confirm the two rejection reasons:\\n  - `MemoryLedger.changes()` (`core/dist/src/ledger.js`): decode failure, and `after > #stateChanges.length`.\\n  - `SqliteLedger.changes()` (`ledger-sqlite/dist/src/index.js`): decode failure, and `after > #stateChangeHighWater()` — a restored/replaced ledger lowers the high-water mark, so a *stored* cursor from the old databas",
